X86_64 DekkerMfence
(* dekker_broken.kl with an `mfence` between raising the own flag and
   reading the other one. The fence drains the store buffer, so at least
   one thread sees the other's flag up and backs off, or enters only
   after the other has lowered its flag: no lost increment.
   Property: no final state in which both threads entered and an
   increment was lost (counter = 1).
   Proved for every layout of the runner's image, the code verbatim
   (`decls = {}`): `DekkerAbs.x64_dekker_public_verbatim`
   (Promising/SLR/EnvG/DekkerAbsPublic.lean). Proved from the runner's exact
   configuration (declarations and initial memory included):
   `DekkerAbs.x64_dekker_public_runner` (DekkerAbsRunner.lean), with a
   `.Concrete` instance.
   Proved no fault at the runner's declarations (rule-level):
   `DekkerAbs.x64_dekker_public_runner_noFault`
   (Promising/SLR/EnvG/X64NoFaultDLocks.lean), with a `.Concrete` instance.
   The shared symbols are typed (`uint32_t`) so that the runner declares
   them as sized locations at the width of the code's accesses (DECLS): the
   runner's declarations then agree with the proofs'
   locations. The code block and the verdict are unchanged.
   Proved (fault freedom at the runner's declarations, MEMVALS):
   `DekkerAbs.x64_dekker_public_runner_faultFree`
   (Promising/SLR/EnvG/X64FaultFreeFixed.lean), with a `.Concrete` instance.
# flags: -fuel 300
# expect: Never
*)
{
uint32_t flag0=0; uint32_t flag1=0; uint32_t counter=0;
0:rsi=flag0; 0:rdi=flag1;
1:rsi=flag1; 1:rdi=flag0;
}
threads { P0: worker done; P1: worker done; }
code {
worker:
  xorl %edx,%edx
  movl $1,(%rsi)
  mfence
  movl (%rdi),%eax
  testl %eax,%eax
  jne done
  movl $1,%edx
  movl (counter),%ecx
  addl $1,%ecx
  movl %ecx,(counter)
  movl $0,(%rsi)
  jmp done
done:
}
~exists (0:edx=1 /\ 1:edx=1 /\ [counter]=1)
